    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1514</span><span class="rf-text">A peasant rebellion led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyorgy-dozsa">György Dózsa</a> is crushed by the nobles under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> take Nándorfehérvár (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabac">Szabács</a>, leaving most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> indefensible</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1526</span><span class="rf-text">Suleiman leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> at the head of the Ottoman army and advances up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1526</span><span class="rf-text">The Ottoman army defeats the Hungarians near Mohács and King Louis II dies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1526</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> is occupied and burned, and the Ottoman army withdraws to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1541</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> decide to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> after the siege of that year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1976</span><span class="rf-text">The battlefield becomes a national historical memorial site on the 450th anniversary</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-mohacs" data-v="00e6f3b0aa" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies take up their positions</b> The Hungarians stood in two lines on a waterlogged plain, mercenary infantry and artillery in the centre of the first with heavy cavalry on both flanks, and levy infantry and reserve cavalry behind. The Ottomans faced them with Ibrahim Pasha in the centre.</li><li><b>The Hungarian cavalry charges the Ottoman front</b> In the early afternoon Archbishop Pál Tomori led a charge that drove back the Ottoman front line and caused disorder among the irregulars. The Ottoman centre gave ground.</li><li><b>The Ottoman centre falls back and the reserves follow</b> Ibrahim Pasha's centre fell back in a controlled manoeuvre and formed a crescent that exposed the flanks of the advancing cavalry. King Louis and the reserves moved in support, and the Hungarian centre advanced as well.</li><li><b>The Ottoman wings counterattack and the guns open fire</b> The Ottomans struck with cavalry and light infantry from both wings, while Janissaries and artillery fired from prepared positions. The Hungarian army had advanced too far and too fast.</li><li><b>The Hungarian army is encircled and its lines collapse</b> Surrounded and under concentrated musket and cannon fire, the Hungarian army collapsed within two hours. Over 14,000 Hungarian troops died by one estimate.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france.jpg" alt="Francis I of France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:François_Ier_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean Clouet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> in 1490, the Hungarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, who did not want another heavy-handed king, procured the accession of Vladislaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, known as King Dobře, 'all right', for accepting every petition laid before him. He gave most of the royal estates and revenues to the nobility, and the nobles of the parliament cut their tax burden by 70 to 80%. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-army-of-hungary">Black Army</a> of Matthias was dissolved, and border guards and castle garrisons went unpaid. In 1514 a peasant rebellion led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyorgy-dozsa">György Dózsa</a> was crushed by the nobles under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>, and Hungarians were no longer a politically united people.</p><p>In 1521 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Nándorfehérvár, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, the strongest Hungarian fortress on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabac">Szabács</a>, which left most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> indefensible. In 1523 the aristocrats pledged funds for an army of 60,000 and 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> to retake it, but the army, recruited late and without food, disbanded under hunger and disease without making the attempt. King Louis II married Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> in 1522, an alliance the Ottomans saw as a threat. The Porte made at least one and possibly two offers of peace, which Louis refused for unclear reasons.</p><p>In 1525 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>, defeated at Pavia, asked Suleiman to make war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>; the road ran across Hungary, and the request suited Suleiman's ambitions. Louis appealed to the European princes, but only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> offered aid, which arrived in 1527, and the Pope offered 50,000 gold pieces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> did nothing, as the Habsburg armies were in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mohács&#8221; cites where it covers Jagiellonian-Habsburg attempt to organize defence against the Ottomans, Preparation for Mohács:</p><ul><li>Frank Tallett, European Warfare, 1350–1750 (Cambridge University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-zapolya.jpg" alt="John Zápolya" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Szapolyai_J%C3%A1nos_fametszet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Erhard Schön (1491-1542), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hungarians gathered in three units: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>'s Transylvanian army guarding the passes, between 8,000 and 13,000 men; the main army under Louis, with Spanish, German, Czech and Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>; and a smaller force of around 5,000 under the Croatian count Christoph Frankopan. At Mohács they numbered some 25,000 to 30,000. The known division by arms lists 3,000 armoured knights from the noble banderiums, the king's bodyguard of 1,000 armoured knights, 4,500 light cavalry, 6,700 infantry, mainly Hungarian, 5,300 papal infantry, mainly German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknechte</a>, and 1,500 Polish infantry, with an unknown number of artillerymen. They had 85 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a> of the era, some 300 cannons. Their army at Mohács numbered perhaps 50,000, though some contemporary and modern historians say 100,000; another passage gives perhaps 45,000 fighting troops, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, Timarli cavalry and conscripted Balkan levies, and says Suleiman and Ibrahim left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in April with 100,000 men.</p><p>On 26 August, at a conference in Louis's camp at Bata, the chancellor Stephen Brodarics advised waiting for reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, but impetuous nobles persuaded the king to fight at once. The war council did not wait for the Croatian and Transylvanian forces, a few days' march away, and chose an open but uneven plain with marshes. The Hungarians formed two lines: mercenary infantry and artillery in the centre of the first with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> on both flanks, levy infantry and reserve cavalry in the second.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle began on the afternoon of 29 August. The Hungarians were well rested, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had just finished a strenuous march in the summer heat. Confident in their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, the Hungarians hoped to break the Ottoman line by a direct assault. Early in the afternoon Archbishop Pál Tomori led a charge that drove back the Ottoman front line and caused disorder among the irregulars.</p><p>Ibrahim Pasha, who commanded the Ottoman centre, fell back in a controlled manoeuvre, forming a crescent that exposed the flanks of the advancing Hungarian cavalry; contemporaries credited him with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a>. As Louis and the reserves moved up in support, the Ottomans counterattacked with cavalry and light infantry from both wings, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and artillery opened concentrated fire from prepared positions. According to some accounts, Hungarian bullets struck Suleiman's cuirass during the charge. The Hungarians, advanced too far and too fast, were encircled and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire, and within two hours their lines collapsed.</p><p>King Louis tried to flee but drowned in the marshy ground near the Csele stream, weighed down by his armour; his body was found weeks later, buried in the mud. Suleiman is reported to have expressed regret for the young king's death. Among the dead were thousands of Hungarian soldiers and over 1,000 nobles, including seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and twenty-eight barons.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Mohács&#8221; cites where it covers Battle:</p><ul><li>R. J. Overy, A History of War in 100 Battles (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>It is estimated that over 14,000 Hungarian troops died, including 4,000 cavalry and 10,000 infantry, and a further 2,000 prisoners were executed. That evening Suleiman and his staff did not grasp the scale of the victory, believed they had met only part of the Hungarian forces and kept their army under arms all night. Next day he saw he had won a decisive victory. He stayed four more days at Mohács and executed about 2,000 captured Hungarian nobles, whose bodies were found by archaeologists after 2020.</p><p>Queen Mary, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, heard of the defeat on the evening of 30 August and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pozsony</a> with her treasures. The army reached Buda on 11 September; the city surrendered without a fight, but was set on fire on 14 and 15 September together with most of Pest, though orders had been given to spare it. Malkoçoğlu Bali Bey's horsemen murdered the mainly male population with great cruelty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pecs">Pécs</a> was plundered and burned. On 13 September the Turks broke into a camp of refugees between Maróth and Dömös and massacred about 25,000 people, including old people, women and children.</p><p>Suleiman took the treasures of the Matthias era, including most of the Corvinus Library, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he arrived on 13 November, keeping only Pétervárad, Illok and Szalankamen. The Hungarian nobility elected two kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> and Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1541 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> decided to occupy Buda. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, who also held the north and west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; the Ottomans held central Hungary and suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, a principality from 1571.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from a mixed body of writings, and the figures in them do not agree. Stephen Brodarics wrote a report of it in 1527 for the Polish king Sigismund I, Antal Verancsics wrote an account of the events of Louis's reign, and the Ottoman chroniclers Kemalpasazade, Dselalzade, Pecsevi and Szolakzade, together with Suleiman's war diaries, tell the campaign from the Turkish side. Suleiman is only said to have spoken his words of grief over the king's body. Contemporary records of Louis's preference for retreat are sparse.</p><p>The numbers are uncertain. The Ottoman army is put at perhaps 50,000, with some historians at 100,000, and its fighting troops at perhaps 45,000. The Hungarian total at Mohács is 25,000 to 30,000, while the units known to historians, whose data do not allow a reconstruction of the rest of the army, cover fewer men. The dead are put at over 14,000, with a further 2,000 executed prisoners. Some details of the fighting, such as the bullets striking Suleiman's cuirass, appear only in some accounts.</p><p>Historians differ on why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> lost. Fichtner points to a breakdown of communication between Louis and Ferdinand, Alfred Kohler to the king's lack of vigour, and Stephen Fischer-Galati to Louis's failure to grasp the Turkish threat. Louis's refusal of the Ottoman peace offers is unexplained.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohács was a decisive Ottoman victory, achieved through planning, firepower and encirclement, and the beginning of sustained Ottoman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> wars. For all intents and purposes it meant the end of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> as a unified entity, and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Ferdinand's aim was to create a new empire from his kingdoms, officially named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> only in 1804.</p><p>The partition was followed by near constant warfare. Territory held by the Kingdom of Hungary shrank by around 60% during the Ottoman wars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> armies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> several times between 1530 and 1686. Suleiman died of natural causes in Hungary during the siege of Szigetvár in 1566, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> failed at Eger in 1552 and took it only in 1596. The Habsburgs needed Hungary's economic power for these wars; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> remained as important economically as the Austrian lands and was Ferdinand's largest source of revenue.</p><p>In Hungarian memory Mohács is a national trauma and the turning point in the country's history, summed up in the saying Több is veszett Mohácsnál, 'more was lost at Mohács'. Hungary would only regain its former boundaries in the 19th century, with full independence from Habsburg rule coming after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The battlefield beside the village of Sátorhely became an official national historical memorial site in 1976, on the 450th anniversary, with a memorial by the architect György Vadász; a reception hall and exhibition building by him was completed in 2011.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Mohács on 29 August 1526 the Ottoman army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> encircled and broke the Hungarian army of King Louis II, who drowned in flight. The kingdom was later partitioned, and Hungarians remember the battle as a national tragedy.</em></p>

